   <front>
     <title>More Comments on the Forthcoming Protocol</title>
     <seriesInfo name="RFC" value="40" stream="IETF"/>
+    <author fullname="E. Harslem" initials="E." surname="Harslem">
+      <organization showOnFrontPage="true">RAND</organization>
+      <address/>
+    </author>
+    <author fullname="J. Heafner" initials="J.F." surname="Heafner">
+      <organization showOnFrontPage="true">RAND</organization>
+      <address/>
+    </author>
     <date year="1970" month="March"/>
     <abstract pn="section-abstract">
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-1">We have recently discussed NWG/RFC Nos. 36 and 39 with Steve Crocker,
+UCLA.  Steve has asked that we elaborate on the errors, queries, and
+HOST status that were mentioned in NWG/RFC #39.</t>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-2">Please voice your opinions soon in order to affect the forthcoming
+protocol specifications.</t>
     </abstract>
-    <boilerplate>
-    </boilerplate>
   </front>
   <middle>
+    <section anchor="error-messages" numbered="false" removeInRFC="false" toc="exclude" pn="section-1">
+      <name slugifiedName="name-error-messages">ERROR MESSAGES</name>
+      <artwork name="" type="" align="left" alt="" pn="section-1-1"><![CDATA[
+     <ERR> <Code> <Command length> <Command in error>
+]]></artwork>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-1-2">&lt;Code&gt; is an eight-bit field that specifies the error type.  The
+assigned codes are shown below.  &lt;Command length&gt; is a 16-bit integer
+that indicates the length of the &lt;Command in error&gt; in bits.  The
+&lt;Command in error&gt; is the spurious command.</t>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-1-3">The ranges of &lt;Code&gt; are shown below in hexidecimal.</t>
+      <dl newline="false" spacing="compact" indent="7" pn="section-1-4">
+        <dt pn="section-1-4.1">00</dt>
+        <dd pn="section-1-4.2">Unspecified error types</dd>
+        <dt pn="section-1-4.3">10-0F</dt>
+        <dd pn="section-1-4.4">Resource errors</dd>
+        <dt pn="section-1-4.5">10-1F</dt>
+        <dd pn="section-1-4.6">Status errors</dd>
+        <dt pn="section-1-4.7">20-2F</dt>
+        <dd pn="section-1-4.8">Content errors</dd>
+        <dt pn="section-1-4.9">30-3F</dt>
+        <dd pn="section-1-4.10">Unused</dd>
+      </dl>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-1-5">Specific values of &lt;Code&gt; are shown below with their meaning.</t>
+      <table align="center" pn="section-1-6">
+        <thead>
+          <tr>
+            <th align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">&lt;Code&gt; value</th>
+            <th align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Semantics</th>
+          </tr>
+        </thead>
+        <tbody>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">00</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Unspecified errors.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">01</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Request for an invalid resource.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">02</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Request for an exhausted resource, try later.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">03-0F</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Unused.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">10</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Invalid &lt;RSM&gt;, i.e., link connected but unblocked.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">11</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Invalid &lt;SPD&gt;.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">12</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Invalid &lt;ASG&gt;, i.e., connected but no &lt;RDY&gt;
+received.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">13</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Message received on blocked link.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">14-1F</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Unused.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">20</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Unknown command code.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">21</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Message received on unconnected link.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">22</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Invalid &lt;RFC&gt;.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">23</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Invalid &lt;CLS&gt;.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">24</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Invalid &lt;RSM&gt;, i.e., link not connected.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">25</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Invalid &lt;FND&gt;.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">26</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Invalid &lt;END&gt;.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">27</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Invalid &lt;RDY&gt;.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">28</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Invalid &lt;ASG&gt;, i.e., not connected.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">29-2F</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Unused.</td>
+          </tr>
+          <tr>
+            <td align="center" colspan="1" rowspan="1">30-FF</td>
+            <td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Unused.</td>
+          </tr>
+        </tbody>
+      </table>
+    </section>
+    <section anchor="queries" numbered="false" removeInRFC="false" toc="exclude" pn="section-2">
+      <name slugifiedName="name-queries">QUERIES</name>
+      <artwork name="" type="" align="left" alt="" pn="section-2-1"><![CDATA[
+     <QRY> <My Socket>
+or   <RPY> <Your Socket> <Text>
+]]></artwork>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-2-2">The &lt;QRY&gt; is the query indicated in NWG/RFC #39 and &lt;RPY&gt; is the reply.
+The format of &lt;Text&gt; is shown below; also refer to NWG/RFC #36, p. 3.</t>
+      <artwork name="" type="" align="left" alt="" pn="section-2-3"><![CDATA[
+<Text>::= <16 bit count of relevant connection table entries>
+          <relevant connection table entries>
+
+<relevant connection table entries>::=
+                                     <relevant connection table entries>
+                                     <a relevant connection table entry>
+                                     <a relevant connection table entry>
+
+<a relevant connection table entry>::= <local socket> <foreign socket>
+                                       <link> <connection state>
+                                       <flow state and buffer control>
+                                       <reconnection control state>
+]]></artwork>
+    </section>
+    <section anchor="host-status" numbered="false" removeInRFC="false" toc="exclude" pn="section-3">
+      <name slugifiedName="name-host-status">HOST STATUS</name>
+      <artwork name="" type="" align="left" alt="" pn="section-3-1"><![CDATA[
+     <NOP>
+]]></artwork>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-3-2">An NCP may be up, down, pending, etc.  When an NCP changes its
+state to UP it should send a &lt;NOP&gt; to each remote NCP which
+indicates the NCP is available.  The sending NCP can then
+construct a vector of HOST status from the RFNMs it receives.  An
+NCP receiving a &lt;NOP&gt; can update the availability of the sending
+NCP in its HOST status vector.</t>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-3-3">[ This RFC was put into machine readable form for entry ]
+[ into the online RFC archives by Richard Ames 6/97 ]</t>
+    </section>
   </middle>
   <back>
   </back>
